<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippocrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 460–c. 370 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hippocrates of Kos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippocrates was a Greek physician from the island of Kos, born around 460 BCE, who is traditionally called the father of medicine. He taught that diseases had natural causes rather than divine ones, and a large body of medical writings, the Hippocratic Corpus, was attributed to him and his followers, though few can be assigned to him with certainty. He is said to have died at Larissa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippocrates.jpg" alt="Hippocrates" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified engraver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hippocrates.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
